# /plan-feature (engineer track)

One feature in, a locked plan out. This runs the planning skills in the order a
train runs them, carries each phase's output into the next, and decides the
things a team decides so a person is only asked about the things a person owns.

It is not a shortcut past the work. Every phase does its full job; what changes
is who answers the questions along the way.

Input: $ARGUMENTS

## Phases, in order. Do not reorder or run in parallel.

Each phase reads the previous phase's artifact. Complete a phase fully, save its
artifact, log its record, then start the next.

**Phase 1 - Decompose.** Follow the `/pi-prep` skill at full depth: read
`~/.claude/skills/pi-prep/SKILL.md` and execute it. Output stories with real
acceptance criteria, enablers, dependencies, ROAM-ready risks, load against
capacity, and proposed objective wording.

**Phase 2 - Challenge scope.** Follow `/feature-review` in the mode given, or
`selective` if none was given. If the decomposition in Phase 1 came out larger
than the stated capacity, use `reduce` instead and say why you overrode the
default.

**Phase 3 - Lock the technical plan.** Follow `/arch-runway`. This is the phase
that can stop the pipeline: a BLOCKED verdict means build has not been cleared
to start, and BLOCKED is a respectable outcome. Do not soften it to keep the
pipeline moving.

**Phase 4 - Test approach.** Follow `/test-plan` against the stories from Phase
1 and the edge cases from Phase 3. Do not rediscover Phase 3's failure modes.

Between phases, print a one-line transition: what came out, what goes in next.

## Deciding as you go

Apply the decision-rights rules above to every question the underlying skills
would have asked. In this pipeline specifically:

- **MECHANICAL and TEAM**: decide, record, and carry on. Story shape, estimates,
  test levels, edge-case selection, automation ordering.
- **PRODUCT OWNER**: never decide. Accumulate these and present them at the gate.
  Scope cuts, acceptance-criteria changes, anything that alters what the team is
  committing to.
- **TRAIN**: never decide, and surface immediately rather than at the gate — a
  cross-team dependency found in Phase 1 changes Phase 2's arithmetic, so
  holding it back would make the rest of the pipeline wrong.
- **CHALLENGE**: if you think the feature itself is the wrong thing to build,
  say so once, at the gate, in the form the decision-rights section describes.

## The gate

Stop at the end and present:

```
## Plan complete: <feature>

### What the plan says
<3-5 sentences: the stories, the shape, the approach>

### Decided for you (N)
| # | Class | Decision | Why |
<MECHANICAL and TEAM decisions, most consequential first>

### Needs your call (N)
| # | Class | Question | Options | Recommendation |
<PRODUCT_OWNER and TRAIN items - these block nothing technically,
 but the plan is not committed until a person answers them>

### Verdict: LOCKED or BLOCKED
<from Phase 3; if BLOCKED, the named blockers>

### Open questions
```

Then ask whether to accept the plan, change any of the decisions, or answer the
escalations. Do not proceed past the gate on your own.

## Do not

- Do not skip a phase because it looks unnecessary. If a phase genuinely does
  not apply, run it far enough to say what you checked and why it does not.
- Do not compress a phase into a summary. A one-line "architecture looks fine"
  is a skipped phase wearing a phase's name.
- Do not decide a PRODUCT OWNER or TRAIN question to keep the pipeline moving.
  An unanswered escalation is a finished pipeline; a quietly-taken decision is
  a broken one.
- Do not let a BLOCKED verdict from Phase 3 become a warning. Blocked means
  build has not been cleared.
